# CONSULT-64 — Adversarial review of the "portal clip test boundary" framing

Reviewer: fresh Fable 5, no session context. Evidence pack read first and treated as given.
Role: attack the leading framing ("boundary/degenerate case in the portal clip test, camera
near a portal plane, inside the shared portal/frustum traversal").

## 0. Verdict up front

The leading framing picked one disjunct of the pack's own open question ("portal clip test
rejecting" vs "camera's CONTAINING CELL resolved wrong") without a single discriminating fact.
Reading the code, the CONTAINING-CELL account is not merely viable — it is structurally the
better fit, and the repo's own git history contains a fact the evidence pack omitted that
substantially reframes the whole investigation (Section 2.1).

---

## 1. The strongest alternative: camera containing-cell desync

### 1.1 How the camera's cell is actually determined (this is the load-bearing mechanism)

- `RenderWorld::drawScene` (RenderWorld.cpp:875-892) takes the cell straight off the camera
  object: `ms_cameraCell = camera.getParentCell(); ms_dpvsCamera->setCell(...)`. dPVS does NOT
  resolve containment; it trusts the engine's assignment absolutely. If the assigned cell is
  wrong, dPVS traverses the portal graph from the wrong root and whole cell-blocks of draws are
  never submitted — exactly the RenderDoc signature (missing DrawIndexed blocks, all shared
  draws EQUAL).

- The chase camera's cell is maintained INCREMENTALLY, not by geometric containment:
  - `RenderWorldCamera` ctor registers `CellProperty::addPortalCrossingNotification(*this)`
    (RenderWorldCamera.cpp:21). FreeChaseCamera → GameCamera → RenderWorldCamera, so the ground
    chase cam is on this path.
  - Every frame, `FreeChaseCamera::alter` re-bases: snaps the camera cell to the PLAYER's cell
    (FreeChaseCamera.cpp:615-617), teleports to the player's transform with portal transitions
    DISABLED (620-637), then applies shoulder offset (`move_o`, 651) and the zoom pull-back
    (`move_o(negativeUnitZ * m_currentZoom)`, 775) with transitions ENABLED.
  - Each enabled `move_o` fires `CellPropertyNamespace::Notification::positionChanged`
    (CellProperty.cpp:110-186), which walks the move SEGMENT through
    `CellProperty::getDestinationCell` → `Portal::testPenetration` (segment vs the portal
    POLYGON, CellProperty.cpp:720-770, Portal.cpp:195-210).

  Consequence: the camera's cell is correct ONLY IF every frame's offset+zoom segment that
  crosses a cell boundary crosses it THROUGH a portal polygon. A segment that crosses the
  boundary through a WALL (no portal polygon there) registers no transition → the camera's
  assigned cell silently diverges from its physical position. The tracker has no self-check
  and nothing logs — consistent with "no errors in logs."

### 1.2 Why recent camera changes feed exactly that degenerate path

Three recently landed, in-tree-documented behaviors put the camera on wall-crossing segments
at doorways — the precise sighting location:

- Shoulder-offset ease (FreeChaseCamera.cpp:675-682, door-snap fix): the comment literally
  says "Brief shoulder-cam wall clip during the ease is accepted." Accepted for near-plane
  clipping — but nobody re-audited what a camera transiently INSIDE the doorframe wall does to
  segment-based cell tracking (it crosses the cell boundary through the wall, not the portal).
- Collision pull-in rate limit (`cs_cameraPullInSpeed`, FreeChaseCamera.cpp:757-770): when a
  collision demands pulling in, the camera now deliberately stays TOO FAR OUT for several
  frames — i.e., at/behind the colliding wall. The comment documents that "occluders ended up
  between the camera and the avatar" in the earlier variant. During the ease the camera's
  physical position and its assigned cell can disagree.
- Zoom recovery lerp (line 611): when the collision clears (player turns at the entrance),
  the camera sweeps back OUT over multiple frames; if the outward path exits beside the portal
  polygon (over the lintel, through the doorframe), again no crossing registers.

All are elapsedTime-scaled: post-zone-in load hitches (large/erratic dt — the very stalls the
recent perf work was fighting) make the ease steps large and the transient positions wild.
That gives the zone-in clustering WITHOUT invoking dPVS registration races.

### 1.3 Why this fits every locked fact

- Renderer/bitness-agnostic: pure shared engine code (sharedObject + clientGame). ✓
- Missing draws never submitted, shared draws EQUAL: wrong dPVS root cell culls whole cells;
  everything else renders identically. ✓ (Pair B, 74 vs 234 draws: camera cell = small interior
  cell while physically elsewhere plausibly yields a tiny visible set. Pair A, 42 missing:
  adjacent-cell subset. Two different wrong-cells / depths — a clip-test boundary does not
  naturally explain two such different magnitudes at the same doorway.)
- Small camera angle change restores INSTANTLY: an orbit chase camera's "angle change" IS a
  position change; next frame re-bases to the player's cell and re-runs crossing segments along
  a new path → cell re-derived correctly → whole visible set returns in one frame. ✓
- Intermittent, self-clearing, no persistence: the tracker is history-dependent BY
  CONSTRUCTION (cell(t) = f(cell(t-1), segment(t))); identical camera poses with different
  approach paths give different cells. A portal CLIP test is memoryless — identical
  (cell, pose) inputs must reproduce identically. Intermittency at the same spot is evidence
  AGAINST a pure clip-test boundary and FOR hidden state, and the biggest documented hidden
  state in this pipeline is the incrementally tracked parent cell. (Caveat: dPVS keeps some
  internal temporal-coherence state even with occlusion off, so this is an argument, not a
  proof.)
- Sightings cluster at the cantina ENTRANCE: doorways are where camera segments graze portal
  polygon edges and where the ease/pull-in transiently embeds the camera in the doorframe. ✓

## 2. Audit of the evidence pack — weakest "locked" facts

### 2.1 The pack omits the decisive historical fact (found in git, not in the pack)

Commit a976f81e2 (2026-07-02, TWO days before the first sighting) REMOVED the interior
chase-cam zoom cap (`freeChaseCameraInteriorMaximumZoom`). The removed code's own comment:

> "Pressed against a back wall, gl05/32-bit re-triggers the borderline portal cell-cull flip
>  (exterior shows through the wall, clears on look up/down ...) ... SWGEmu/retail avoid all
>  of this by simply not letting the indoor camera zoom out to the walls."

Three consequences the pack misses:
1. This artifact CLASS was already known, named ("portal cell-cull flip"), and deliberately
   SUPPRESSED by keeping the camera away from walls/portals indoors. The suppression was
   removed 2026-07-02; first sighting 2026-07-04. The bug almost certainly PREDATES the recent
   fix wave — what changed is EXPOSURE (the camera is newly allowed to press against interior
   geometry), not a regression in the traversal. The pack's "UNKNOWN whether the portal bug
   predates these" is answerable from the repo.
2. The historical flip happened pressed against a BACK WALL — where there is NO portal. A
   portal CLIP test cannot be the gate for exterior-through-a-portalless-wall; a camera
   cell/containment flip can (camera epsilon-past the wall plane → world/exterior cell wins →
   interior culled). The one prior in-repo observation of this class is thus
   containment-flavored, not clip-test-flavored.
3. Retail/SWGEmu never fixed this — they FRAMED it away. That is a strong prior that the
   defect is a structural containment quirk, not a numeric boundary someone else solved.

### 2.2 "A SMALL camera angle change restores it" — non-discriminating

The pack (and the leading framing) implicitly reads this as "camera pose crossed back over a
clip-plane boundary." But in a chase camera, angle change = position change = new crossing
segments = full cell re-derivation next frame. BOTH hypotheses predict instant restore. This
fact has zero discriminating power and should not anchor the framing. Its only real content is
the memoryless-vs-stateful argument (1.3), which cuts AGAINST the clip test.

### 2.3 "No asset/portal/mesh errors in logs" — void, not exculpatory

Nothing in the containment path logs anything: a missed `testPenetration` crossing is a silent
`return true` (CellProperty.cpp:131-132,164-168), wrong parent cell raises no warning,
`drawScene` never validates `ms_cameraCell` geometrically. Log silence eliminates nothing.

### 2.4 "Sightings cluster shortly after zone-in" — exposure/sampling bias

Shortly after zone-in is exactly when the player is AT the cantina entrance. Plus dt spikes
amplify the eases (1.2). This fact cannot convict the phased WorldSnapshot load; it barely
even correlates with it once the doorway-exposure confound is admitted.

### 2.5 The FPU_PRESERVE precedent points at shared math, not at the clip test specifically

The deterministic gl05/32-bit instance was fixed by restoring x87 precision to "shared
portal-visibility math." `Portal::testPenetration` → `IndexedTriangleList::collide` (the
containment/crossing test) is exactly as much "shared portal-visibility math" as the dPVS
clip test. The precedent is neutral between the framings; the team is using it as if it
localized to the clip test.

## 3. Other alternatives considered — and honestly killed

- Object-to-cell assignment wrong / objects never added to the cell
  (`inWorldAddDpvsObject`, RenderWorld.cpp:627-654): killed by the good capture seconds later
  at a slightly different angle — the objects ARE registered and render fully; their dPVS cell
  state did not change between the pair. Object assignment is camera-independent; an
  angle-only restore is impossible under this account.
- Phased WorldSnapshot / budgeted preload leaving dPVS registrations incomplete: same kill —
  registration state cannot flip with camera angle within one session-second. Survives only as
  an indirect dt-spike amplifier for the containment account (1.2). Also, first sighting
  (2026-07-04 per pack) does not postdate the phased-load commit by enough to separate it from
  the 2026-07-02 zoom-cap removal — and the zoom-cap removal has the documented mechanism.
- Door state driving portal passability (`closedStateChangedHookFunction`,
  RenderWorld.cpp:754-762 disables the portal's dPVS object when closed;
  `Portal::testPenetration` returns false when DISABLED): a real mechanism, but door state is
  proximity/animation-driven, not camera-driven; restore-by-camera-angle across 5 sightings
  coinciding with door-state flips is implausible. Also doorless portals get
  `m_alwaysOpen = true` (Portal.cpp:269). Secondary at best — worth one glance at whether the
  cantina entrance portal has a door style, nothing more. NOTE the sinister variant though:
  a DISABLED portal is also invisible to `testPenetration`, so any transient disable would
  ALSO break cell tracking — this variant collapses back into the containment account.
- Per-render exclusion list leak (`disableDpvsObjectForThisRender` / `ms_excludedDpvsObjects`):
  would persist across camera changes; killed by instant restore.

## 4. THE decisive probe (cheap, armed for days, logs only on anomaly)

Cross-check the camera's ASSIGNED cell against an independently RE-DERIVED containing cell,
every frame, and log one line only on mismatch.

Where: end of `RenderWorld::drawScene` setup (RenderWorld.cpp, right after line 876), or the
end of `FreeChaseCamera::alter`.

How (re-uses existing engine code, ~a handful of portal segment tests per frame at a doorway,
zero heap):
1. Anchor = player object position + player parent cell (server-authoritative via
   `handleNetUpdateTransformWithParent`, ClientController.cpp:511 — the most trusted cell in
   the client).
2. Walk the segment anchor→camera-position through the same
   `CellProperty::getDestinationCell(start, end, t)` chain the crossing notification uses
   (loop shape of CellProperty.cpp:156-176).
3. Compare the walked terminal cell with `camera->getParentCell()`.
4. On mismatch only, log: frame #, assigned cell name/index, walked cell name/index, camera
   pos_w, player cell + pos_w, `m_currentZoom`, `m_colliding`, whether the pull-in rate limit
   clamped this frame, dt, and last frame's submitted-draw count. Optionally also trigger on a
   frame-over-frame draw-count collapse (>40% drop with camera delta <0.5 m / <5 deg) to
   time-stamp visible breakage even if the mismatch check somehow passes.

Decision rule — decisive in BOTH directions:
- Sighting occurs AND a mismatch line brackets it → containing-cell desync CONVICTED; the
  clip-test framing is dead; the fix hunt moves to the camera cell tracker (and the probe's
  `m_colliding`/rate-limit bits will name the feeder).
- Sighting occurs AND the probe is silent → the containment alternative is DEAD, cheaply, and
  the clip-test framing has earned its status for the first time (right now it has no
  discriminating evidence at all).

Cost: a few segment-vs-portal tests per frame (portal lists at the cantina are tiny), one
fprintf on anomaly. Runs for days. No behavior change (probe only reads).

Secondary confirmation (only if the probe convicts): re-add the interior zoom cap via its old
cfg knob for one soak — if sightings stop, exposure via a976f81e2 is confirmed and the
containment defect can be fixed under a stable repro recipe (zoom out against the entrance
wall from inside).

